  <title>Introduction to Machine Learning: Gradient Descent and Neural Networks</title>
  <dc:creator>Yanhong Wan</dc:creator>
  <link>https://rainbowbow123.github.io/posts/Neural_Network/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p>To understand machine learning, one must have a basic understanding of optimization. In a learning problem, a loss function is specified to evaluate estimation accuracy, and the goal of optimization is to minimize this loss. Specifically, a loss function assigns a scalar value to model predictions as a quantitative measure of estimation error. Common examples include:</p>
<p>Mean Squared Error (MSE): <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L_%7B%5Ctext%7BMSE%7D%7D%20=%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bn%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5En%20(%5Chat%20y_i%20-%20y_i)%5E2"></p>
<p>Mean Absolute Error (MAE): <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L_%7B%5Ctext%7BMAE%7D%7D%20=%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bn%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5En%20%7C%5Chat%20y_i%20-%20y_i"></p>
<p>For binary classification, cross-entropy is commonly used: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L_%7B%5Ctext%7BCE%7D%7D%20=%20-%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bn%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5En%20%5Cleft%5By_i%5Clog%20%5Chat%20p_i%20+%20(1-y_i)%5Clog(1-%5Chat%20p_i)%5Cright%5D"></p>
<p>Below is an example of the loss function of MSE.</p>
<pre><code>import numpy as np 

y_true = np.array([10,20,30])
y_pred = np.array([12,18,27])

loss = np.mean((y_true-y_pred)**2 )
print(loss)</code></pre>
<p>Once a loss function is specified, the next question is how to minimize it. This is handled by optimization algorithms. We introduce the most fundamental algorithm - gradient descent here.</p>
<p>For a differentiable function, the gradient points in the direction of steepest increase. Minimization therefore proceeds by updating parameters in the opposite direction of the gradient. The step size of each update is controlled by the learning rate, whereas an excessively large learning rate may cause overshooting and divergence, and an excessively small learning rate leads to slow convergence.</p>
<p>Below is an example of gradient descent for a linear regression <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Chat%20y%20=%20w%20x"> with loss <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L%20=%20(y_%7B%5Ctext%7Btrue%7D%7D%20-%20wx)%5E2">. The gradient with respect to <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?w"> is <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%7B%5Cpartial%20L%7D/%7B%5Cpartial%20w%7D%20=%20-2x%20(y_%7B%5Ctext%7Btrue%7D%7D%20-%20wx)">. Change the learning rate in the code below to see how it affects the optimization process. (It is generally good around 0.004. I have to change from Python to ObservableJS to make it interactive here.)</p>
