<p>So far, the model is a si mple linear regression. Next, we upgrade this regression model to a neural network. A neural network contains an input layer, one or more hidden layers, and an output layer. Each layer applies a linear transformation followed by an activation function.</p>
<p>For a generic layer,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?z%20=%20W%20x%20+%20b,%5Cquad%20h%20=%20%5Cphi(z)">,</p>
<p>where <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cphi(%5Ccdot)"> denotes an activation function. Common choices include ReLU, sigmoid, and tanh. ReLU preserves positive inputs and truncates negative ones. Sigmoid and tanh map real-valued inputs into bounded intervals.</p>
<p>ReLU: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cphi(z)%20=%20%5Cmax(0,%20z)">. Sigmoid:<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cphi(z)%20=%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B1%20+%20e%5E%7B-z%7D%7D">. Tanh:<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cphi(z)%20=%20%5Cfrac%7Be%5E%7Bz%7D%20-%20e%5E%7B-z%7D%7D%7Be%5E%7Bz%7D%20+%20e%5E%7B-z%7D%7D">. Softmax (for vector-valued input <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?z">):<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cphi(z_j)%20=%20%5Cfrac%7Be%5E%7Bz_j%7D%7D%7B%5Csum_k%20e%5E%7Bz_k%7D%7D">.</p>
<p>All these activation functions introduce nonlinearity。 Without activation functions, stacking multiple layers would still result in a linear model. Like in linear regression, we can also use gradient descent to optimize the parameters of a neural network. The gradients can be computed using backpropagation, which applies the chain rule of calculus to propagate gradients from the output layer back to the input layer. For example, we consider a neural network with 2 hidden layers. The input <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x"> is transformed through the first hidden layer to produce <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?h_1">, then through the second hidden layer to produce <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?h_2">, and finally to the output <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?y">.</p>
<p>Forward:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0Az_%7B1,i%7D%20&amp;=%20W_1%20x_i%20+%20b_1,%20%5C%5C%0Ah_%7B1,i%7D%20&amp;=%20%5Cmathrm%7BReLU%7D(z_%7B1,i%7D),%20%5C%5C%0Az_%7B2,i%7D%20&amp;=%20W_2%20h_%7B1,i%7D%20+%20b_2,%20%5C%5C%0Ah_%7B2,i%7D%20&amp;=%20%5Cmathrm%7BReLU%7D(z_%7B2,i%7D),%20%5C%5C%0A%5Chat%7By%7D_i%20&amp;=%20W_3%20h_%7B2,i%7D%20+%20b_3.%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D"></p>
By the chain rule, backpropagation gives: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20L%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20W_1%7D%0A=%0A%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20L%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20%5Chat%20y%7D%0A%5Ccdot%0A%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20%5Chat%20y%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20h_2%7D%0A%5Ccdot%0A%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20h_2%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20z_2%7D%0A%5Ccdot%0A%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20z_2%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20h_1%7D%0A%5Ccdot%0A%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20h_1%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20z_1%7D%0A%5Ccdot%0A%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20z_1%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20W_1%7D.">The goal is still to minimize the MSE, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L%20=%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bn%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5En%20(%5Chat%20y_i%20-%20y_i)%5E2">. For this network, the loss is differentiable with respect to all parameters, so gradients can be written in closed form using the chain rule. Specifically, for ReLu, the derivative is defined in a piecewise manner, [ (z) =
<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cbegin%7Bcases%7D%0A1,%20&amp;%20z%20%3E%200,%20%5C%5C%0A0,%20&amp;%20z%20%3C%200,%0A%5Cend%7Bcases%7D">
<p>] and a subgradient at ( z=0 ) is typically chosen by convention. This leads to the indicator term ( (z&gt;0) ) appearing in the gradients.</p>
<p>We analytically write out the gradients for all parameters below.</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20L%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20W_3%7D%0A=%0A%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bn%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5En%20(%5Chat%20y_i%20-%20y_i)%5C,%20h_%7B2,i%7D%5ET">,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20L%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20b_3%7D%0A=%0A%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bn%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5En%20(%5Chat%20y_i%20-%20y_i)">,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20L%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20W_2%7D%0A=%0A%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bn%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5En%0A(%5Chat%20y_i%20-%20y_i)%5C,%0AW_3%5C,%0A%5Cmathbf%7B1%7D(z_%7B2,i%7D%3E0)%5C,%0Ah_%7B1,i%7D%5ET">,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20L%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20b_2%7D%0A=%0A%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bn%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5En%0A(%5Chat%20y_i%20-%20y_i)%5C,%0AW_3%5C,%0A%5Cmathbf%7B1%7D(z_%7B2,i%7D%3E0)">,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20L%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20W_1%7D%0A=%0A%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bn%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5En%0A(%5Chat%20y_i%20-%20y_i)%5C,%0AW_3%20W_2%5C,%0A%5Cmathbf%7B1%7D(z_%7B2,i%7D%3E0)%5C,%0A%5Cmathbf%7B1%7D(z_%7B1,i%7D%3E0)%5C,%0Ax_i%5ET">,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20L%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20b_1%7D%0A=%0A%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bn%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5En%0A(%5Chat%20y_i%20-%20y_i)%5C,%0AW_3%20W_2%5C,%0A%5Cmathbf%7B1%7D(z_%7B2,i%7D%3E0)%5C,%0A%5Cmathbf%7B1%7D(z_%7B1,i%7D%3E0)">.</p>
<p>In the next post, I will use these analytically derived gradients to perform optimization for this network in Python.</p>



